@* What the operator sees when a screen could not continue. Rule 7 is the bar: a failure
   produces something a reader can act on, never a stack trace, and never silence. So the
   underlying message is shown rather than a generic apology, and the way out is a control
   rather than an instruction to restart the app.

   A separate component rather than markup inlined into Routes.razor's ErrorContent, so the
   fallback can be rendered and asserted on its own. Recovery is the caller's, because only
   the caller holds the ErrorBoundary this content is standing in for. *@

<section class="screen-error">
    <h1>That screen could not continue</h1>

    <p class="what">@Error.Message</p>

    <p class="advice">
        Nothing further was written by the step that failed. Anything already saved is still
        on disk. Go back and try again; if it happens again, send this message along with the
        file you were working on.
    </p>

    <button class="recover" @onclick="OnRecover">Back to the start</button>
</section>

@code {
    [Parameter] public Exception Error { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnRecover { get; set; }
}
